<br /><img width="64" height="64" src='./avatars/anamasinger.gif'><br />                <td bgcolor="whitesmoke" width="100%" colspan="2" valign="top">
                  <table width="100%" height="100%" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
                    <tr>
                      <td valign="top">
                      <img src="http://www.eciunbroken.com/forums/images/icon_mi_1.gif" width="15" height="15" border="0" alt="" title="" hspace="3" /><font color="midnightblue" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica" size="1">Posted&nbsp;-&nbsp;11/21/2003&nbsp;:&nbsp; 12:20:54 PM</font>
                      &nbsp;<a href="pop_profile.asp?mode=display&id=4"><img src="http://www.eciunbroken.com/forums/images/icon_profile.gif" width="15" height="15" border="0" alt="Show Profile" title="Show Profile" align="absmiddle" hspace="6" /></a>
                      &nbsp;<a href="post.asp?method=TopicQuote&TOPIC_ID=97&FORUM_ID=36"><img src="http://www.eciunbroken.com/forums/images/icon_reply_topic.gif" width="15" height="15" border="0" alt="Reply with Quote" title="Reply with Quote" align="absmiddle" hspace="6" /></a>
                      <hr noshade size="1"></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
           	<td valign="top" height="100%"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica" size="2" color="midnightblue">
                      <font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica" size="2" color="midnightblue"><span class="spnMessageText" id="msg"> As the attempt on the Vindicator reveals... our healers need to do some reading beforehand to smooth out some tactics...<br /><br />Yes, I "borrowed" this text...<br /><br />"I'd given this to Figment, so I still have it. <br /><br />Repost:<br /><br />Hi gang. Our ch rotation needs some work, so i'm going to give a run down of what to do.<br /><br />A) Someone (typically the senior cleric) will set up the rotation. In general you want your most experienced (with raids, not levels) first to kick things off. You then set the remaining order by Level alternating low/high. The reason for this is the lower levels will likely go oom first, and if you spread them out you reduce the risk of having 2 consecutive oom clerics. For Example:<br /><br />1) Leader<br />2) lvl 55<br />3) lvl 61<br />4) lvl 54<br />5) lvl 58<br /><br />In this case, the 54 is likely oom first, but havinig the 61 and 58 flank them, the cycle tightens without much problem. The 55 then goes oom and again the cycle tightens by 1 step.<br /><br /><br />B) Generally you will want 3-5 clerics in cycle, on a 4 second rotation. Any extra clerics will serve one of 2 uses. They are either the gap cleric (more later) or the Rampage cleric. If you are rampage cleric, you should make hotkeys for any and all rampage tanks (also, the Raid window allows for quick targeting) and do your best to keep them healed, typically with HoTs. <br /><br />C) Gap cleric. f you are a gap cleric, then when you see someone miss their count, you launch a ch in their place. The gap cleric should Never cancel a CH, even if you see the person you are covering for wake up/unlag and throw theirs. The reason is too many times 2 clerics have launched a ch at the same time, they both cancel, and the tank Dies. Additionally, try to keep a HoT running on the Tank<br /><br />D) Counting. some clerics are lazy (hehe, me too) and they start looking just for the person in front of them (#4 looking for #3 message). You need to be counting for Every heal. Get in that habit "1...2...3...*heal message* 1...2...3 MY heal 1...2...3... *heal message*". If for no other reason, this will keep you in rhythm. If you just look for the guy before you "message , *i should go* 1...2...3..." then that timing to recognize its your turn can skew the cycle.<br /><br />E) Missed Cleric. If a cleric misses then the person following that cleric throws a ch. ONLY the cleric following (and the gap if available) should do this. Otherwise you get:<br /><br />&lt;(1)&gt; ch on %t<br />1...<br />2...<br />3...<br />4...<br />5...<br />&lt;(4)&gt; ch on %t<br />&lt;(3)&gt; ch on %t<br />&lt;(5)&gt; ch on %t<br /><br />Now we have 3 ch's going,good chance they all get canceled, and Nobody knows who should be next, so we then get<br />1...<br />2..<br />3...<br />&lt;(4)&gt; ch on %t<br />&lt;(5)&gt; ch on %t<br /><br />Thats 3 wasted ch's, half a cycle, or an extra 12 seconds we could have kept the tank up. Or worse they get canceled, tank goes down, and we LOSE.<br /><br />F) Dead, LD, or OOM clerics. If a cleric is removed OR they drop 2 heals in a row then the cycle tightens. No need to try to change your message, but #4 will throw in #3s place. Everybody else will follow suit. Its the gap clerics job to recognize when the cycle is tightened, so they don't need to fill for the dropped cleric.<br /><br />G) Mana. Couple things<br />i) Mod rod early, that way you can mod rod late. <br />ii) After the first 2 cycles (depending on mob) you can sit between calls. IF you sit, then make sure you stand at the count of 2 not at 4, otherwise there is a delay that makes you on a 4.5-5 sec cycle, and the tank dies. <br />iii) If you have Yaulp V, use it. Have as much mana regen up as you can (BAotR + KEI = 20 right there, Yaulp V makes it 30)<br />iv) when you hit 20% mana, announce it in Raid chat and heal chat. Once do not spam. Hopefully a friendly necro can feed you<br />v) when you go OOM Announce in heal chat 3 quick times "Cleric (#) is OOM, tighten the cycle".. Then start medding. As mana allows, toss small gap heals. Do NOT use your ch button, as that will create confusion. If #4 is already covering for #3 because of the oom message, then sees the #3 ch message, then #4 won't know if they really oom or what to do. And uncertainty will kill you fast.<br /><br />H) Druid. In the event that there are not enough clerics, a druid can be used. There are 2 options. You can have 2 druids act as 1 cleric in rotation, so Dr00d_01 and Dr00d_02 will both ch on #4 slot. Or, you can have them at regular spot, but the cleric following is on half rotation (2 sec rather then 4). Both are much less efficient as it removes the druid's ability to nuke, dot, debuff (like telling the rogue to stand in front)<br /><br />I) Tank switch. In the event that the MT dies (its going to happen), the cycle shifts to the SA. The procedure is this:<br />i) the first cleric (or lowest # cleric depending on who's alive with mana) cancels whatever spell they had, /target SA, and call the &lt;(1)&gt; Complete Heal %t<br />ii) all other clerics do /assist on the mob and lauch small quick heals on whoever is getting beat on. Watch for the lead cleric to resume the rotation on new tank.<br />iii) once it is your turn in the new cycle, cancel any heals and falll back into rotation EVEN IF SA DOES NOT HAVE AGRO YET<br />iv) eventually the SA will pull agro and the cycle will already be in place to keep him/her alive<br /><br />What this does is it lets the clerics do a bit of healing to try to keep folk alive, BUT once the cycle is going again, if someone still has agro, then you MUST let them die. It also keeps 4 clerics from all trying to resume the cycle and nobody knowing whose turn it is.<br /><br />I think that sums up everything I wanted to say. I want EVERY cleric that has read this to respond, even if its just "I read it" That way I know what clerics I need to bitc... er, tell to come check the boards. If you have any questions, post them here. <br /><br />Eleena Transient <br /><br />Eleena, former cleric of EQ"<br /><br />Gwendowlyn</span id="msg"></font>
           	</td>
                    </tr>
